{
  "concept": "In y = mx + b, the slope m controls a line's steepness and direction while the intercept b sets where it crosses the y-axis, so any line on a graph can be matched to its equation by reading those two numbers off the picture.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Cartesian grid with light gray unit gridlines spanning x from -7 to 7 and y from -5 to 5",
    "Horizontal x-axis with arrowheads both ends, labeled 'x', ticks and numerals -7 through 7",
    "Vertical y-axis with arrowheads both ends, labeled 'y', ticks and numerals -5 through 5",
    "Line I: orange, very steep positive slope (about m = 3, b = 0.5), arrowheads both ends, label 'I' near its top exit just right of the y-axis",
    "Line II: light blue, shallow positive slope (about m = 0.5, b = 3), arrowheads both ends, label 'II' at upper right near x = 4",
    "Line III: teal, moderate positive slope (about m = 2, b = -3), arrowheads both ends, label 'III' at upper right, crosses x-axis near x = 1.5",
    "Line IV: dark blue, negative slope (about m = -2, b = 3), arrowheads both ends, label 'IV' at lower right near x = 4",
    "Candidate line: dashed magenta/purple movable line y = mx + b controlled by the sliders, arrowheads both ends",
    "y-intercept marker dot on the candidate line at (0, b) with coordinate callout",
    "Rise-over-run staircase markers (horizontal run segment and vertical rise segment with labeled lengths) drawn on the selected line when clicked",
    "Readout box showing the candidate line's equation y = mx + b with current values",
    "Legend / color key pairing each Roman numeral with its color"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "slopeSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That the sign of m decides whether the line rises or falls and the magnitude of m decides how steep it is, while the pivot point at (0, b) never moves.",
      "label": "Slope m of candidate line",
      "range": [-4, 4, 0.25],
      "default": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "interceptSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That b slides the whole line vertically without changing its direction, so two lines with the same steepness are told apart only by where they cut the y-axis.",
      "label": "y-intercept b of candidate line",
      "range": [-5, 5, 0.5],
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "selectLine",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "That the visual steepness of a given line is literally rise over run, converting the picture into a numeric slope that can be matched to an equation."
    },
    {
      "id": "showCandidate",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That hiding the reference lines forces the learner to predict m and b from the equation, and showing them confirms or refutes the match.",
      "label": "Reference lines",
      "options": ["Show I–IV", "Hide I–IV"],
      "default": "Show I–IV"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Four lines, four equations",
      "narration": "Four lines cross the same grid: orange I is steepest, light blue II is the shallowest, teal III rises from -3, and dark blue IV is the only one falling. Your job is to read each line's slope and intercept.",
      "state": { "showCandidate": "Show I–IV", "slopeSlider": 1, "interceptSlider": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Rotate the candidate line",
      "narration": "Drag the slope slider and watch the dashed line pivot about the origin. Large positive m gives a steep climb like line I; small positive m gives a shallow climb like line II.",
      "state": { "slopeSlider": 3, "interceptSlider": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Negative slope flips the direction",
      "narration": "Push m below zero and the dashed line tips the other way — only line IV has this downhill behavior, so its equation must have a negative slope.",
      "state": { "slopeSlider": -2, "interceptSlider": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Lift the line with b",
      "narration": "Now raise b to 3. The line keeps its downhill tilt but slides up until it crosses the y-axis at 3 — it lands exactly on line IV. Slope plus intercept pins a line uniquely.",
      "state": { "slopeSlider": -2, "interceptSlider": 3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same intercept, different slope",
      "narration": "Keep b = 3 and set m = 0.5. The line still passes through (0, 3) but now leans gently upward onto line II — proof that b alone cannot identify a line.",
      "state": { "slopeSlider": 0.5, "interceptSlider": 3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Match line III",
      "narration": "Click line III to see its rise-over-run staircase, then set m = 2 and b = -3. The dashed candidate overlays it, confirming y = 2x - 3.",
      "state": { "slopeSlider": 2, "interceptSlider": -3 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Test yourself",
      "narration": "Hide lines I–IV and try to reproduce line I from memory: a steep positive slope with a small positive intercept. Show them again to check.",
      "state": { "showCandidate": "Hide I–IV", "slopeSlider": 3, "interceptSlider": 0.5 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A line is completely determined by two readable numbers — how fast it climbs (m) and where it cuts the y-axis (b) — so matching graphs to equations is just reading steepness and crossing point off the picture.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Square-ish coordinate plane with a pale gray unit grid, black axes with double arrowheads, x labeled -7..7 and y labeled -5..5, axis letters 'x' at right and 'y' at top. Four full-width straight lines in the source palette: orange (steepest, ~y = 3x + 0.5, label I near the top just right of the y-axis), light blue (~y = 0.5x + 3, label II at upper right), teal (~y = 2x - 3, label III at upper right, x-intercept near 1.5), dark blue (~y = -2x + 3, label IV at lower right). Each line carries arrowheads at both ends and its Roman numeral in matching color at the end where it exits the grid. Overlay a dashed magenta candidate line driven by the sliders, with a filled dot at (0, b) and an equation readout 'y = mx + b' in the corner. Clicking a line thickens it, dims the others, and draws a right-triangle staircase (horizontal run in gray, vertical rise in the line's color) with the run and rise values labeled plus the resulting slope fraction. Keep the grid subordinate visually so the four colored lines and the dashed candidate read clearly."
}

